 Here is a Semgrep rule that matches the vulnerable behavior and avoids matching the patched behavior:

```yaml
id: "vuln-gmail-js-a83436f4"
title: "Potential XSS in Gmail API parsing function"
description: "This rule detects potential Cross Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities in the Gmail API parsing function. It checks for the use of `Function` to parse JSON data, which can be used to execute arbitrary JavaScript code."
severity: WARNING
language: javascript
type: problem
pattern: >-
  function parse_response(response) {
    var parsedResponse = [],
        data, dataLength, endIndex, realData;

    try {
      response = response.replace(/\n/g, " ");
      response = response.substring(response.indexOf("'") + 1, response.length);

      while (response.replace(/\s/g, "").length > 1) {
        dataLength = response.substring(0, response.indexOf("[")).replace(/\s/g, "");
        if (!dataLength) {
          dataLength = response.length;
        }

        endIndex = (parseInt(dataLength, 10) - 2) + response.indexOf("[");
        data = response.substring(response.indexOf("["), endIndex);

        var get_data = new Function("\"use strict\"; return " + data);
        realData = get_data();

        parsedResponse.push(realData);

        response = response.substring(response.indexOf("["), response.length);
        response = response.substring(data.length, response.length);
      }
    } catch (e) {
      console.log("Gmail post response parsing failed.", e);
    }

    return parsedResponse;
  }
metadata:
  tags: ["xss", "eval"]
```

This rule will match if the `parse_response` function uses the `Function` object to parse JSON data. It will not match if the function correctly parses JSON using `JSON.parse`. This is a generalization of the security mechanism without depending on unnecessary repository-specific names or functions.

